{
  "schemaVersion": 4,
  "id": "t02-006",
  "track": "track-02",
  "title": "ASP.NET Core Minimal APIs route handlers endpoint filters validation and results",
  "status": "source-backed-scope-complete",
  "concepts": [
    "Minimal APIs expose HTTP endpoints through route-handler delegates and endpoint-routing conventions. They use the same server, middleware pipeline, dependency injection, routing, and HTTP abstractions as controller applications.",
    "Route groups share a prefix and metadata across related handlers. Endpoint filters wrap handler invocation for cross-cutting behavior near the handler; middleware wraps the larger request pipeline.",
    "Parameter binding infers sources using type and route metadata. Explicit binding attributes make ambiguous or security-sensitive sources clear; body binding is constrained because one request body can be consumed only once.",
    "Handlers can return framework results, typed results, strings, or serializable values. Typed results make response types explicit and improve compile-time and API-description behavior.",
    "Validation is model-boundary work, not a replacement for domain invariants. ASP.NET Core 10 added built-in Minimal API validation support; .NET 11 adds asynchronous DataAnnotations validation APIs. Distinguish framework behavior from application validation policy."
  ],
